{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Displate for Supporting PBS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3200,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Antimatter drives sound like science \nfiction, but they may not be as far as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7120,
    "dDurationMs": 11680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you think. There’s a version that could, \njust maybe, launch within your lifetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone—before we get started two quick \nannouncements. We wanted to let your help with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "likes and commenting is working! Thanks \nto liking and commenting, the algorithm  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30560,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is sharing current episodes \nwith more of our community,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34080,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so keep it up! And if you haven’t yet, subscribe \nand hit the bell so you don’t miss new episodes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40640,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Next, If you believe in humanity’s propensity \nfor interstellar travel, we’ve got the perfect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shirt for you. It fuses the Alcubierre Warp \nDrive with the X-Files “I Want to Believe”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50320,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "poster—and thanks to the UV printing it glows \nafter sun exposure even in the depths of space.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s 10% off for the first 48 hours. Link \nin the description. Now, on to the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64160,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Warp speed, hyperdrive, jumpdrives, \nspace folding, subspace, wormholes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68160,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite improbability drives… We’re pretty \ngood at coming up with ways to travel faster  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than light, at least in fiction. Sadly, Einstein’s \nrelativity theory tells us it’s almost certainly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible to travel faster than light, at least \nfor macroscopic objects over meaningful distances.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Probably our future exploration of the galaxy \nwill have to take the slow road. That said,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can certainly do a lot better than the current \ngeneration of chemical rockets, which are still  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "burning stuff for energy, you know, like a \nsteam train. In terms of energy per kilogram"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the antimatter drive is near as \nefficient as you can get within  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bounds of the laws of physics, \nif you care about those. As such,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111680,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its also a staple of science fiction, and \nit also feels as far of as the warp engine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there have been lots of advances in recent \nyears that warrant this update on the timeline  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of antimatter-powered space travel. And there are \nearly versions that you might even see launch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with the requisite antimatter review. \nWe’ve talked about antimatter before about how, in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1928, Paul Dirac discovered it in his equations as \nhe tried to bring quantum mechanics into agreement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142960,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with special relativity and found negative energy \nelectron. And how it in 1932 it was discovered"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in reality when Carl Anderson noticed \ncosmic ray electrons curving the  wrong  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way in a magnetic field they proved to \nbe Dirac's anti-electrons—positrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what is antimatter really? It’s pretty fair \nto describe it as the mirror image of regular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter, and every particle of matter has an \nantimatter counterpart. It's the Bizarro to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Superman, the Wario to Mario, the Dirac \nto Feynman. The same but oh so different."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Antimatter is inevitable in a universe with the \nsymmetries of ours. If we think of particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as swirls and vibrations in the quantum \nfields, well, those fields can swirl and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189120,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vibrate backwards? The laws of physics \nare almost completely symmetric if we,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say, flip all quantum charges to be their \nopposites, or reflect to their mirror image,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201520,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or if we reverse the flow of time. Antimatter is \nthis—charge-reversed and mirror reflected matter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208640,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which can equivalently be thought \nof as time-reversed matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The matter-antimatter symmetry means it’s possible \nto produce particles matter out of a pure vacuum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as long as you also produce the corresponding \nparticles of antimatter to balance. And also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as long as you have the energy to account for the \nnew mass, according to Einstein’s equation E=mc^2. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231760,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This process is called pair production. But if the \nlaws of physics are symmetric in time this process  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239120,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "works backwards. A matter-antimatter pair can also \nbe un-created—annihilated—to produce energy. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again, E=mc^2, so take the mass, multiply it \nby the speed of light, which is a big number,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253040,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then multiply it by the speed of light \nagain, and you get a very, very big number. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s why antimatter is the ultimate \nenergy-dense spaceship fuel. For example  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "annihilate an espresso with \nan anti-espresso and you'll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268000,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get an explosion with the \npower of the modern h-bomb."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are of course complications to using \nanti-matter or we'd be flying around with it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and blowing each other up with it already. It's \nvery difficult to make the stuff in quantity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283520,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even more difficult to \nstore. And it's also complicated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287680,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to harness it in rocket propulsion.\nBut before we can talk about solving those we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to dispel a misconception. There’s a commonly \nrepeated statement that matter and antimatter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "annihilation releases ‘pure energy.’ There’s \nnot really any such thing as pure energy. Energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306880,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a property possessed by systems. It can take \nmany forms—mass energy, kinetic, potential, etc. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most charitable interpretation of “pure \nenergy” is energy that’s easily accessible and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "usable. Mass-energy is the least pure or “free” in \nthat sense, and that's the price of it being the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325280,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most compact form of energy. And that mass energy \nisn’t necessarily all liberated in annihilation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333840,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That process will often produce other massive \nparticles, so some energy remains locked away.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341040,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea of this process producing “pure energy” \nis probably from electron-positron annihilation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that case, the ingoing particles have such \nlow masses that the energy they produce typically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "isn’t enough to produce other particles. \nInstead they annihilate into photons, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358080,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the rare neutrino. The entire energy content of \nthose photons can be captured pretty efficiently."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365840,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to accelerate our spaceship we \nreally need momentum, not energy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Basically, we need to throw stuff out the back \nof our ship as fast as possible so that momentum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377600,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conservation accelerates us forwards. Although \nour annihilation photons have plenty of energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are massless and so carry relatively \nlittle momentum. Also, very high energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons are hard to direct efficiently. Ideally \nwe want to harness whatever energy we produce  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to blast massive particles out behind us. It’s \ncertainly possible to do that with the energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403760,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these electron/positron annihilation photons in \nan indirect manner, and I’ll come back to options."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s another issue with \nthis “high efficiency” type of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414480,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "annihilation and that has to do with \nstorage, which I'll also come back to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418560,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But all of this is nudging \nus towards using more massive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422880,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "versions of anti-matter to power our spaceship."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Massive anti-particles are much harder to \ncreate, but let’s see where we’re at with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431840,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this. So we had position in 1932. \nAnd the next anti-thing discovered"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438720,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was 2000 times heavier, the anti-proton in \n1955. Followed quickly by the antineutron And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then we started to produce antinuclei with \nanti-protons and anti-neutrons together.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451920,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First anti-deuterium then anti-tritium then \nantihelium-4. More exotic anti-nuclei followed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458640,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the current record \nbeing anti-hyperhydrogen-4."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462480,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of these heavier particles are identified in \nthe debris of high-energy particle collisions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468800,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which means they’re both rare and difficult \nto capture. There are two steps to this:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475760,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first slow the particle down then trap \nit. These collision products start out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving fast—often at a good fraction of light \nspeed--and they’re moving in a random direction.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So they need to be channeled and then slowed. \nThe world leaders in this antimatter trapping  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495360,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are at CERN’s Antiproton Decelerator. It’s an \nanti-accelerator for capturing anti-protons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504160,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once slowed, the antiparticle needs \nto be trapped. This is arguably the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "harder part. If antimatter touches matter it \nannihilates, which means there’s no such thing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as an antimatter-resistant material \ncontainer that isn’t itself made of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antimatter. The solution is a non-material \ncontainer, which means a force field,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the electromagnetic field offers good options \nfor both charged and non-charged antimatter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most famous containment device for \ncharged antimatter is the Penning trap,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536400,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which electrodes at either end of the \ntrap create an electric field that keep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540560,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charged particles in the middle along the axis,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543360,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while a magnetic field along the axis prevents \nany from drifting away in the radial direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550000,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the technology used \nby the BASE collaboration,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who have managed to store a hundred antiprotons \nfor a full year in their larger Penning trap."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560080,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electromagnetic containment like this \nrelies on the anti-particle having a net  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564800,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge. But that introduces a new problem. \nLike charges repel, and so end up as a big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573600,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diffuse cloud—which defeats the point of using \nantimatter as a compact energy-dense fuel. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580400,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you want to keep the particles \nclose together you need a colossal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "EM field with a similarly colossal anti-matter \ncontainment device again defeating the point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This why we can't just us positrons \nor just anti-protons as fuel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Happily there’s a work around. If you trap \nboth positrons and antiprotons in, for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602000,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a Penning trap, and then cool them, they’ll \ncombine into anti-hydrogen atoms. Of course, now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have the problem that the resulting anti-atom \nis electrically neutral and so immediately escapes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615520,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Penning trap, which is presumably a bad \nthing. A more sophisticated trap is needed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The current trick is to use the fact that the \nanti-hydrogen atom has a magnetic moment—like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627040,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a little bar magnet. It’ll try to align and move \nin the direction of a magnetic gradient. So if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634880,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have a magnetic field with a minimum value \nin all 3 dimensions, any anti-hydrogen in that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field will move towards the minimum and get stuck \nthere. This is called a magnetic minimum trap."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because the new anti-hydrogen will immediately \nescape its Penning trap after formed, the magnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652800,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minimum trap has to be superimposed \ndirectly over the Penning trap. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the magnetic minimum trap is far weaker \nthan the Penning trap. And so anti-hydrogen  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666000,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needs to be really cold, colder than around 1 \nKelvin to have a chance of remaining trapped.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Various novel methods like laser cooling \nare used to further chill this antihydrogen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But despite best efforts, this whole process \nis still very inefficient, with only a tiny  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fraction of produced anti-protons and positrons \nbeing converted to captured anti-hydrogen. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s also difficult to keep the much of \nthis stuff trapped for long. The current  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "record is by the ALPHA collaboration. \nThe team trapped 112 antiatoms for times  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ranging from one-fifth of a second to up \nto 1,000 seconds.Not exactly long enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706400,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for an interstellar journey. But now that we have \nactual anti-hydrogen, a new possibility opens up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anti-hydrogen is expected to behave \nessentially identically to regular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hydrogen. And that means that if we \nhave enough of it it can undergo phase  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723920,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transitions into more useful forms. Hydrogen \nfreezes at 14 Kelvin at atmospheric pressure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730960,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so the hope is that enough antihydrogen \nwould solidify at that point. That’s the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dream actually— antihydrogen ice fuel \npellets suspended in magnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we have our antimatter fuel. \nLet’s look at how we can use it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bring a hydrogen and antihydrogen together \nand first the electron and positron annihilate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "producing high-energy gamma rays. The \nfollowing proton-antiproton annihilation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is way more complicated. That’s because we’re \nreally annihilating three quarks with three  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765280,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antiquarks. When the hydrogen and antihydrogen \ncome together, the individual quarks don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767760,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all annihilate. That only happens to the \nfirst quark and antiquark to make contact.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re likely to produce particles like a gluon \nor W boson, which in turn creates more quarks,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780000,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fragmenting the two baryons into a shrapnel of \nparticles like pions. Sure enough, this is exactly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787680,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how antiprotons were discovered in collision \nexperiments by Segre and Chamberlain in 1955."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795200,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These pions give us another approach at antimatter \npropulsion. They can be used directly as our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“working mass”, which means the stuff you throw \nout the back for momentum exchange, your thrust.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This design is called a ‘pion rocket’. It’s \npossible because the charged pions can be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "channeled by a magnetic field. Unfortunately, \nthe energy released in hydrogen-antihydrogen  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "annihilation also ends up as neutral pions, gamma \nrays, and neutrinos that ignore our magnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field. If we really don’t want to waste the \nenergy from these, there other ways to capture it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One approach is to capture the photons and \nkinetic energy of the particles to generate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electricity. That electricity can then be used \nto accelerate other particles to provide thrust,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843520,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as in an ion drive, which we already employ \nfor very steady, if slow acceleration in space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851120,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "craft. That can be done whether or not \nthe annihilation products are also used  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for propulsion. And that makes it also an \noption for electron-positron annihilation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so how long before we get our first \nantimatter craft? Well, quite a while  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for our first crewed interstellar craft, but \nmaybe not so long for our first unmanned probe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially for our own solar system. That \nmight be possible if we use antimatter in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a sort of hybrid mode with \nnuclear fission or fusion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now nuclear power is proposed in a \ncouple of space travel scenarios. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more prosaic it to use a fission or fusion \nreactor generating electricity to power an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ion drive. A more radical, but actually \nperfectly within our technological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grasp is nuclear pulse propulsion \nlike the Orion project. In these,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904320,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a series of fission or fusion bombs are detonated \nbehind the ship or behind a sail to propel it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912000,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both direct nuclear reactor and nuclear pulse \npropulsion suffer from the same challenge—the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920640,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size of the device needed. Fusion reactors \nneed to be huge to sustain a reaction with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928000,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "net-positive output, and this is why we don’t \nyet have a commercial fusion reactor. Nukes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936240,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to be big in order to initiate their chain \nreaction. But if a small amount of antimatter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be employed to kick off the reaction, \nthen these devices can be made much smaller."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s take the H-bomb example. A key \ncomponent of the hydrogen bomb is some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sort of fission core—typically a ball \nof plutonium. This is surrounded by a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958960,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layer of heavy hydrogen. The plutonium \nis detonated as a fission bomb,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "providing the heat and neutrons needed \nto ignite fusion in the hydrogen. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967520,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because of the need for a critical mass \nof plutonium, there’s a minimum size for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a hydrogen bomb, which means a minimum explosive \noutput. That in turn means that any Orion-type  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978640,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spaceship needs to be pretty huge to capture and \nwithstand that energy. But what if we replace some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or all of the plutonium core with a tiny grain \nof antimatter? Then we can build a much smaller  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "device of the same style, with the annihilation of \nthe antimatter providing the energy and particle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996640,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bombardment needed to ignite a smaller fission \ncore, or even directly ignite fusion. Either way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this enables a more manageable thermonuclear \nexplosion, which would work on a smaller craft."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011200,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This approach is called antimatter-catalyzed \nnuclear pulse propulsion, and more generally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antimatter-catalyzed fusion or fission \nmay be useful in powering spacecraft  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in various ways. Really, the main \nadvantage of doing this that we need  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far less antimatter because most of the \nenergy is from the “classical” nuclear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fuel. The amount of antimatter needed is \nas low as micrograms by some estimates.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’ll take us a few decades to produce that \nmuch antimatter at current rates, but then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can build an antimatter-catalyzed fission \ncraft that can reach the Oort cloud—further  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than anything we've ever set out there— and \nthat would be in a mere 10 years travel time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058560,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or so at least one proposal has calculated. \nOther proposals for non-crewed craft are also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within distant, but not sci-fi-future \nlevel reach. It’s remotely possible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the first antimatter-enabled launch \nwill be in our lifetimes! For some of us,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anyway. And not of all of this is just \ntheoretical. Early experiments have shown  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that antiprotons cause extreme amplification \nof fission in non-critical heavy metal samples."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090640,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we started out wanting a not-too-disappointing \nalternative to the impossible FTL drives. What’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it really going to take to build a proper sci-fi \nantimatter drive? The main holdup is the rate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at which we can produce and store antimatter. \nOnce we can make enough to solidify, it gets a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1111120,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bit easier with the storage. At current rates \nthis is going to take centuries to millennia."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we can build more and bigger \ncolliders that are devoted just to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antimatter harvesting. But there’s also \nthe possibility of harvesting it in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space is full of radiation and high energy \ncosmic rays, with high energy collisions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1135680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "making antiprotons all the time. The PAMELA \nsatellite discovered that Earth’s magnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141040,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field confines antiprotons and positrons \nproduced by those collisions. So maybe we can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "harvest this antimatter in the Van Allen \nbelts to fuel up for an interstellar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "journey. A side advantage to harvesting in \nspace is safety. I’m not sure I’d want to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blasting off from Earth’s surface on top of \na stash of delicately suspended antimatter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1164240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, that’s where we stand with antimatter \ndrives. They’re not quite around the corner,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1170640,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also there are plausible paths to the first \nversions. And at least for those versions the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1177680,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technical challenges seem pretty solvable. Our \nfirst crewed, interstellar antimatter-powered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1184720,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spaceship is not in our lifetime, but if we want \nit to happen it will. One day we can cross the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1192320,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy by annihilating the reflections\nin the quantum symmetries of spacetime. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1199760,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Displate for Supporting \nPBS. Displate Posters are vibrant,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1204160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high-resolution metal prints, including thousands \nof images from JWST and NASA. If you love  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1209440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "engineering, Displate offers their blueprint \ncollections—ranging from Saturn V rockets to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1214640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "imagined designs like Star Trek’s Enterprise \nor Star Wars’ Star Destroyer. Some feature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1219680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Displate’s Textra 3D posters with textured \nsurfaces, contouring, and selective gloss.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1226080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They can be bought as collectibles to \nstart a collection or as gift ideas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1231200,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each one attaches with an \nincluded  magnet kit—no drilling,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1234000,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no holes—which works for owners or renters. \nIf you want to switch it up, the magnets work  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1238160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with any of Displate’s 2 million+ artworks and \nif you want a more personalized option there’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1243920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the new Custom Displates. With Custom Displate \nyou just upload a photo, artwork, or graphic,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1250400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get an instant preview, and you can turn any \nimage into a high resolution metal poster.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1255360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right now, by using the code SPACETIME you can \nget 33% off your order of 1-2 Textra displates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1261600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or 38% off your order of 3 \nor more Textra displates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1265920,
    "dDurationMs": 16640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s details and a link description \nif you want to explore more."
    } ]
  } ]
}
